# Set and Data

Braces write two different things, and the colon is the discriminator:

> Braces without `:` denote sets. Braces containing key/value entries denote
> `Data`. The empty `Data` literal is `{:}`.

```hql
{}                        // Set<Never>, the empty set
{1, 2, 3}                 // Set<Int>
{"a", "b"}                // Set<String>

{:}                       // Data, the empty record
{name: "Alice"}           // Data
{name: "Alice", age: 42}  // Data
```

A set is values in braces; `Data` is key/value structure in braces. The empty
forms keep exactly that distinction, so no literal takes its meaning from the
type expected of it.

## Empty forms

`{}` is a `Set<Never>`: a set with no elements, of no element type yet. Like
every `Set<Never>`, a declared set type accepts it:

```hql
x : Set<Int> = {}
d : Data = {:}
```

`d : Data = {}` is a type error, since `{}` is already a set:

```text
type error: `d` is declared Data but its value is Set<Never>
```

The constructor writes the same empty set with its element type,
`Set<Int>([])`, which is how an empty set of a known type prints so that the
text reads back as the same value. The empty `Data` prints as `{:}`, alone or
nested: `{a: {:}}`.

## What each is

A `Set<T>` is a collection of distinct values with no positions; see the
[collection model](types/collections/collection-types.md). `Data` is an open
tree whose keys belong to whoever wrote them; see [[data-type]]. A record
literal is not a declared record type: `{name: "Alice"}` is `Data`, and reading
`.name` from it gives an `Option<Data>`.

The rule is specified by [HQL-0029](../../proposals/HQL-0029/README.md), and the
[braces examples](../../../examples/braces/README.md) run it.
